---
// Mount point for lesson 10's change desk. The intro screen is server-rendered
// so the lesson still says something without JavaScript, and so the opening copy
// lives in one place. Everything after the start button is built at runtime by
// src/scripts/courses/changes/changes.ts.
//
// This runs after the prose has said what a Deployment holds and before the prose
// says what happens when you change part of it, because that consequence is the
// exercise. The five requests are a real week's worth of asks and none of them is
// phrased as a question about the template.
import "@styles/changes.css";
---

<div data-changes>
  <div class="cd not-prose my-8">
    <div data-cd-stage>
      <div class="cd-shell">
        <div class="cd-body">
          <div class="cd-intro">
            <p>
              Five things the team asks for over one week, against one Deployment
              that's already serving. Make each change, apply it, and see what it
              did to the pods that were running.
            </p>
            <div class="cd-foot">
              <button
                type="button"
                class="cd-btn"
                data-tone="primary"
                data-go="start">Start</button
              >
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <noscript>
      <p class="cd-note mt-3">
        This exercise needs JavaScript. The short version: a Deployment holds a
        description of a pod and a count of how many. Change anything in the
        description and every pod is replaced, because creating and deleting pods
        is the only thing a Deployment does to one. Change anything else and the
        pods carry on untouched. A label and an image are the same size of event.
      </p>
    </noscript>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import "@scripts/courses/changes/changes";
</script>
